What this solution actually does: it is a local supervisor Windows Service for the two SWIFT *incoming* MQ workers. Every timer tick (interval from the external service configuration, 10 s fallback) it reads the current system date and 14 weekday start/end parameters (HORA_OP_SWF_LU_INI ... HORA_OP_SWF_DO_FIN) from TOMI, asks the shared library whether today is a business day, compares the machine's local time (truncated to the minute) with today's window, and then, in parallel, starts the Windows services CMS-Swf_IN_MQ and CMS-Swf_IN_MQ_MX when it is a business day inside the window, or stops them otherwise. Each start/stop uses ServiceController on the local machine and waits up to 30 s. Control failures are logged and written as TOMI alert rows. At the end of every tick it marks the controller's row as terminated in TOMI. It does not read, parse or route SWIFT messages and does not move funds; it is an availability dependency for the two incoming-message services.
