    serviceController.Start();
if (Status != Stopped && Status != StopPending)                       // Stop path
    serviceController.Stop();
Start is attempted for every status other than Running, including StartPending, ContinuePending, PausePending, Paused and StopPending. ServiceController.Start() on a service that is not stopped raises InvalidOperationException (documented behavior; not exercised here - verify in a controlled environment). The catch then logs an ERROR and inserts a TOMI alert row on every tick until the state settles (GEN-10 pattern). The Stop path likewise issues Stop() while the service is StartPending/Paused without a defined policy.
Code block (csharp)
switch (sc.Status)
{
    case Running: return ResultadoTransicion.SinCambio;
    case StartPending: sc.WaitForStatus(Running, timeout); return ResultadoTransicion.Aplicada;
    case StopPending: return ResultadoTransicion.SinCambio;   // retry on next tick
    case Paused: sc.Continue(); sc.WaitForStatus(Running, timeout); return ResultadoTransicion.Aplicada;
    default: sc.Start(); sc.WaitForStatus(Running, timeout); return ResultadoTransicion.Aplicada;
}
Fix/benefit: explicit per-status policy removes tick-by-tick alert spam and makes restarts deterministic. Test each status with an IServiceControl abstraction (see SICM-A8). Suggested JIRA: SICM-A2 (P1).
