5. Architecture-Fit Note
Apply the standing architecture question in _ORCHESTRATION.md (./_ORCHESTRATION.md). This workload is a bounded periodic local-SCM reconciliation loop (read schedule -> decide -> start/stop two local services), not a continuous message listener and not a multi-step saga. A Windows Service is a reasonable fit because it must act on the *local* Service Control Manager; none of the findings is caused by the hosting model.
Option	Fits when	Trade-offs / unknowns
Modernize current service in place	Local SCM control and existing operations remain required	Lowest disruption; needs the fixes below, tests and a packaged shared library
Modern .NET Worker on Windows	Approved runtime lifecycle and the shared Framework 4.8 library can be migrated	Better hosting APIs; blocked while MUFG_T24 and SimpleInjector remain Framework-only
Scheduled task / SCM recovery + service dependencies	The 30-second reconciliation can be replaced by two daily start/stop jobs	Simpler lifetime; loses auto-correction when someone stops/starts a service manually
Event/message-driven supervision	Reliable lifecycle events and scale justify a broker	Adds correctness surface for what is two polling decisions; not automatically safer
Serverless	A secure agent can perform privileged SCM work	Cannot manage this host's SCM directly; adds a security/availability boundary
Microservice / full rewrite	A separate ownership/scale/isolation requirement exists	Highest cost; not justified by code quality alone

Sequence: fix definite defects (SICM-A1/A2/A3/H1) -> make the build reproducible and add real tests (SICM-H2, A4) -> remove duplication (SICM-A9; GEN-4) -> then evaluate hosting. Unknown and needed from the owners: SLAs for the two MQ services, the real SCM service names, TOMI configuration values, time zone/holiday authority, weekend policy, the service account's SCM rights, and budget.
